SDK TimerQueue: block in kevent instead of yield-spinning (perf pass 2026-10-11, stage 1b).
Applied by tools/build_sdk_from_source.py (SDK_BEHAVIOUR_PATCHES). FABLE2_TIMER_QUEUE_BLOCKING=0
restores the disruptorplus spin wait.

The dispatch thread waited for the next due timer or a newly queued one with
spin_wait_strategy::wait_until_published(timeout): 10 rounds of `yield` instructions, then
std::this_thread::yield() 19 times and sleep_for(1 ms) once, repeated until the timeout. On
macOS that is thousands of swtch_pri syscalls per second (30-45% of the thread's samples in
the 2026-10-11 audit) and timers fired up to ~1.5 ms late (sleep_for leeway).

On Apple platforms the thread now checks for published items without blocking, then sleeps in
kevent on a one-shot EVFILT_TIMER (NOTE_CRITICAL, microseconds) for the next due time plus an
EVFILT_USER event that QueueTimer triggers when the thread is asleep (Dekker-style sleeping_
flag, seq_cst on both sides; EV_CLEAR keeps a trigger pending until the next kevent, so a wake-up
cannot be lost). Timers fire at their due time within tens of microseconds instead of up to a
millisecond and a half late; callbacks, ordering and the flood mitigation are unchanged.

